export default "## Why Fibroid Bleeding Is Worth Tracking Specifically\n\nHeavy menstrual bleeding from fibroids is one of the most common reasons people seek gynecological care. It is also one of the areas where patient-reported data most directly changes what a provider can offer.\n\nUltrasound shows the size and location of fibroids. It does not show how much they are affecting your daily life, whether you are developing anemia, whether the bleeding is getting worse, or what your quality of life looks like over a typical month. That information comes from you, and having it documented makes the difference between a productive appointment and one where the provider is working from a general complaint.\n\nThis guide is for educational purposes only and is not a substitute for medical advice.\n\n## How Fibroids Cause Heavy Bleeding\n\nThe uterus sheds its lining during menstruation through a combination of prostaglandin-driven contractions and a precisely regulated local clotting process that prevents bleeding from becoming excessive. Fibroids interfere with both.\n\nSubmucosal fibroids, which protrude into the uterine cavity, disrupt the surface of the lining and affect local clotting factors. The lining cannot shed and contract normally over a distorted surface. Larger fibroids of any type increase the total area of lining that must be shed, simply producing more blood loss. Fibroids within the muscle wall may reduce the myometrium's ability to contract and compress blood vessels after shedding, contributing to prolonged heavy flow.\n\nThe consequence is often:\n- More days of heavy bleeding per cycle\n- Larger clots\n- Longer periods overall\n- Occasional spotting between periods, particularly with submucosal fibroids\n\n## Recognizing Anemia From Fibroid Bleeding\n\nChronic heavy menstrual bleeding is a leading cause of iron deficiency anemia in people with a uterus. The body loses iron faster than diet can replace it. Symptoms of anemia that can develop alongside fibroid bleeding include:\n\n- Fatigue that seems disproportionate to your circumstances\n- Shortness of breath during mild exertion\n- Difficulty concentrating\n- Pallor (particularly visible in the inner eyelids or nail beds)\n- Cold intolerance\n- Heart palpitations\n\nIf you have significant heavy bleeding and any of these symptoms, ask your provider to check your hemoglobin and iron levels at your next appointment. Untreated iron deficiency anemia has its own significant effects on quality of life and cognitive function.\n\n## What to Track\n\n### Daily protection use\n\nThe most objective data point you can collect is how quickly you saturate your period protection. Note the type of product (pad, tampon, cup, disc), its absorbency level, and how many hours it lasted before it was soaked through.\n\nA pad or tampon lasting less than two hours at peak flow is worth reporting. So is needing to use both a pad and a tampon at the same time. So is waking at night to change. \"I go through a lot\" is less useful than \"I filled a super tampon in under an hour on days 2 and 3 for four consecutive cycles.\"\n\n### Clot size and frequency\n\nNote when you pass clots, how often, and their approximate size. Small clots (smaller than a dime) are less clinically notable than large ones (quarter-sized or bigger). A log showing that you regularly pass multiple large clots gives a provider a much more specific picture than verbal description.\n\n### Period duration\n\nCount total cycle days and distinguish heavy days from light days. A period lasting 8 days with 4 heavy days is different from one lasting 4 days with 4 heavy days, even though both produce the same total bleed time.\n\n### Spotting between periods\n\nNote any bleeding or spotting that occurs outside your expected period window. Submucosal fibroids are a common cause of intermenstrual spotting. Capture the date, duration, and flow level for each episode.\n\n### Functional impact\n\nRecord any days you could not exercise, go to work, attend social events, or feel comfortable leaving the house without extra protection nearby. Log any nights you had to set an alarm or woke spontaneously to change protection. These functional impacts are relevant clinical data.\n\n### Symptoms of anemia\n\nIf you experience fatigue, shortness of breath, heart palpitations, or cognitive fog that tracks with your heavier cycle days or immediately after, note that alongside your period log.\n\n## What to Bring to Your Appointment\n\nA three-to-six-month log that captures the above elements tells a provider:\n\n- How much blood you are losing per cycle, in approximate but concrete terms\n- Whether the bleeding is getting worse, staying stable, or improving\n- Whether your daily life is meaningfully affected\n- Whether there are symptoms suggesting anemia\n\nThis changes the conversation from \"my periods are very heavy\" to a documented pattern of specific severity. Providers can use this to calibrate urgency, decide between watchful waiting and active treatment, and monitor whether a treatment is working.\n\nIf you have already had an ultrasound showing fibroids, bring the report or the images if you have them. Pairing imaging results with a bleeding log gives the fullest possible picture before treatment decisions are made.\n\n## Treatment Context\n\nTreatment for fibroid-related heavy bleeding ranges from medication (hormonal contraception, tranexamic acid, NSAIDs, GnRH agonists) to minimally invasive procedures (endometrial ablation, uterine fibroid embolization) to surgery (myomectomy, hysterectomy). The right option depends on fibroid size and location, bleeding severity, desire for future pregnancy, and individual preferences.\n\nYour tracking data is relevant to all of these decisions. Knowing the exact severity of your bleeding helps determine urgency; knowing whether it is getting worse over time affects the timeline for intervention; knowing how much it affects daily life is part of assessing treatment benefit versus risk.";
